and in psychology also people
talk about an aha moment

00:53:21.720 --> 00:53:23.253
or something like this.

00:53:24.537 --> 00:53:27.930
But it strikes me that this is not

00:53:27.930 --> 00:53:32.930
the only form of creativity
and that we're also creating

00:53:34.050 --> 00:53:39.050
and also use intellect, not
just by this kind of intuition,

00:53:39.180 --> 00:53:43.110
but ways of figuring out these things.

00:53:43.110 --> 00:53:47.850
And that brings us closer,
the creativity closer

00:53:47.850 --> 00:53:51.060
to what a computer can do.

00:53:51.060 --> 00:53:54.930
But I think that this is also
related to what I talked about

00:53:54.930 --> 00:53:59.930
earlier about holism in that
one very important aspect

00:54:00.900 --> 00:54:04.770
in creativity, I think,
and in figuring things out

00:54:04.770 --> 00:54:09.180
that we can always switch
perspective, we can always go

00:54:09.180 --> 00:54:12.690
beyond the place where
we are and look at it

00:54:12.690 --> 00:54:14.280
and look at other things

00:54:14.280 --> 00:54:19.280
and connect them and may try
to figure out things this way.

00:54:19.535 --> 00:54:23.390
So the dynamics maybe of
human creativity is different

00:54:23.390 --> 00:54:27.513
from the dynamics of computers but-

00:54:29.250 --> 00:54:31.800
- But I think that, so
in physics, for example,

00:54:31.800 --> 00:54:36.800
we increasingly know that
scientists own narrative

00:54:37.800 --> 00:54:42.060
of their discoveries are highly incomplete

00:54:42.060 --> 00:54:47.060
and they don't quite reflect
the range of activities

00:54:48.990 --> 00:54:51.030
because we are so fixated on this idea

00:54:51.030 --> 00:54:55.023
of the lone male genius
and this aha moment,

00:54:55.950 --> 00:55:00.950
this eureka moment, that
our own personal recounting

00:55:03.360 --> 00:55:06.183
of our own discoveries is not reliable.

00:55:07.260 --> 00:55:12.260
But what appears to be a
robust fact across questions,

00:55:13.440 --> 00:55:18.440
across individuals is the
fact that the insight,

00:55:18.870 --> 00:55:21.660
if you really drill down into what is new,

00:55:21.660 --> 00:55:26.660
what was the leap, so it turns
out that the creative leap

00:55:27.630 --> 00:55:31.890
appears to be the ability to connect

00:55:31.890 --> 00:55:35.400
different domains of knowledge

00:55:35.400 --> 00:55:39.750
which appear entirely unrelated at first.

00:55:39.750 --> 00:55:41.640
So we are not just talking of tools,

00:55:41.640 --> 00:55:42.960
you're not talking of language,

00:55:42.960 --> 00:55:46.260
we are not talking of
methodologies of investigation

00:55:46.260 --> 00:55:51.260
or anything, but really the
formal representation of ideas

00:55:52.770 --> 00:55:56.790
and properties of objects,
in the case of physics,

00:55:56.790 --> 00:55:58.650
across domains

00:55:58.650 --> 00:56:02.550
and that it's a language
translation exercise, if you will,

00:56:02.550 --> 00:56:04.761
that is more than just the language,

00:56:04.761 --> 00:56:07.270
it's a conceptual domain mapping

00:56:08.580 --> 00:56:11.550
that we do when new discoveries are made.

00:56:11.550 --> 00:56:15.570
And you are right, if
inherently that is a key part

00:56:15.570 --> 00:56:18.000
of what we call human creativity,

00:56:18.000 --> 00:56:20.460
that is hard to replicate in a computer

00:56:20.460 --> 00:56:22.980
because what we feed in

00:56:22.980 --> 00:56:25.500
is what the computer operates out of.

00:56:25.500 --> 00:56:27.870
So you would ultimately
need to have a computer

00:56:27.870 --> 00:56:31.770
that has a training set in
every domain of knowledge

00:56:31.770 --> 00:56:36.770
and then see if it can do
the kind of connection making

00:56:37.590 --> 00:56:41.880
and synthesis that we
believe that human minds

00:56:41.880 --> 00:56:43.923
have the capacity to do.

00:56:47.850 --> 00:56:50.370
- Priya, I don't know if this
is a good moment to discuss

00:56:50.370 --> 00:56:53.250
one of the differences between Gila and me

00:56:53.250 --> 00:56:56.490
and the question she raised at the end.

00:56:56.490 --> 00:57:01.490
Okay, so just to very
briefly summarize it,

00:57:02.100 --> 00:57:04.770
Gila and I agree on this
very important claim

00:57:04.770 --> 00:57:06.093
that logic is infinite,

00:57:07.200 --> 00:57:09.420
and we give similar sorts
of arguments for it,

00:57:09.420 --> 00:57:13.980
but she founds it on another layer,

00:57:13.980 --> 00:57:18.333
this idea of formal laws and
formal features of the world.

00:57:19.680 --> 00:57:22.140
Now, I don't disagree with that.

00:57:22.140 --> 00:57:24.330
It's not that I agree with it either,

00:57:24.330 --> 00:57:27.930
is I am undecided as to whether
to say something like that.

00:57:27.930 --> 00:57:30.030
And I have three sources of hesitation.

00:57:30.030 --> 00:57:32.940
So I'll mention what those
sources of hesitation are.

00:57:32.940 --> 00:57:35.190
The first is really
more of a pragmatic one.

00:57:35.190 --> 00:57:37.620
I think for dialectical purposes,

00:57:37.620 --> 00:57:41.190
if we wanna convince people
that logic is infinite,

00:57:41.190 --> 00:57:45.033
then adding a metaphysical
layer, if you like,

00:57:46.024 --> 00:57:48.540
might be a step too far for some people,

00:57:48.540 --> 00:57:49.680
that's not a reason against it,

00:57:49.680 --> 00:57:52.470
it's just a dialectical point

00:57:52.470 --> 00:57:55.230
and why it doesn't feature
heavily in my book.

00:57:55.230 --> 00:57:58.470
Because getting people today
to agree that logic is infinite

00:57:58.470 --> 00:58:00.960
is already quite a big task.

00:58:00.960 --> 00:58:04.367
The second source of hesitation is that

00:58:04.367 --> 00:58:06.570
on the standard conception of logic,

00:58:06.570 --> 00:58:09.180
model theoretical conception,

00:58:09.180 --> 00:58:13.980
the models that we quantify
over when we consider

00:58:13.980 --> 00:58:17.670
whether the premises entail
the conclusion we formalize,

00:58:17.670 --> 00:58:20.460
those in the logic and
we consider the models

00:58:20.460 --> 00:58:22.650
of the premises and see
whether all those models

00:58:22.650 --> 00:58:24.540
are also models of the conclusion,

00:58:24.540 --> 00:58:26.910
those models standard understanding

00:58:26.910 --> 00:58:29.370
do not include the whole universe

00:58:29.370 --> 00:58:32.280
because they're sets and
they're not the class

00:58:32.280 --> 00:58:35.010
that is the whole universe.

00:58:35.010 --> 00:58:38.610
So in a way it's a little
strange to couple that,

00:58:38.610 --> 00:58:41.370
combine that standard understanding,

00:58:41.370 --> 00:58:46.320
which is a standard understanding
which can be adopted

00:58:46.320 --> 00:58:48.668
on the infinite view of logic as well.

00:58:48.668 --> 00:58:51.960
It's strange to combine that
perhaps uneasy to combine that

00:58:51.960 --> 00:58:56.470
with the idea that logic is
about the formal features

00:58:57.690 --> 00:59:00.960
of reality, if reality's not
actually one of those models.

00:59:00.960 --> 00:59:04.920
And then the third source of hesitation.

00:59:04.920 --> 00:59:08.550
And it's not disagreement,
it's just hesitation

00:59:08.550 --> 00:59:11.250
about going that far, I wanna stress,

00:59:11.250 --> 00:59:14.050
is I'm not sure how much explanation

00:59:16.410 --> 00:59:18.270
of the fact that logic is infinite

00:59:18.270 --> 00:59:20.550
by adding this layer of saying that

00:59:20.550 --> 00:59:22.440
the world has four more features.

00:59:22.440 --> 00:59:27.440
It seems to be redescribing
the fact of logic's infinity

00:59:27.870 --> 00:59:32.460
in more metaphysical
language, laws and structures

00:59:32.460 --> 00:59:33.930
and so on.

00:59:33.930 --> 00:59:37.200
I'm not saying it doesn't
go further, it does seem to,

00:59:37.200 --> 00:59:40.230
but it seems quite close to
what we were saying before

00:59:40.230 --> 00:59:42.990
to really be a powerful explanation of it

00:59:42.990 --> 00:59:44.730
as opposed to a redescription.

00:59:44.730 --> 00:59:47.130
So those are my three
sources of hesitation, Gila.

00:59:47.130 --> 00:59:50.370
I don't disagree with you,
I just don't go quite as far

00:59:50.370 --> 00:59:52.650
for those reasons.

00:59:52.650 --> 00:59:55.833
- Yeah, well, I understand very much

00:59:55.833 --> 00:59:59.010
your first pragmatic consideration,

00:59:59.010 --> 01:00:02.370
it's perfectly reasonable,

01:00:02.370 --> 01:00:06.000
but I do have something to
say about the other reason.

01:00:06.000 --> 01:00:10.200
Now, the second reason is
something that was raised

01:00:10.200 --> 01:00:12.363
for example, by Howie Trifeel.

01:00:13.470 --> 01:00:18.470
And for those of you who are
maybe not familiar with this,

01:00:18.690 --> 01:00:23.690
neither one of us talked
about models very much so far.

01:00:27.030 --> 01:00:32.030
But there is the standard way
of understanding logical truth

01:00:33.450 --> 01:00:36.870
and logical consequence is to say that

01:00:36.870 --> 01:00:40.440
there is a totality of models.

01:00:40.440 --> 01:00:45.440
Each model represents either
a way our language could be

01:00:45.980 --> 01:00:48.333
or a way the world could have been.

01:00:50.700 --> 01:00:55.700
But the non-logical properties
change from model to model,

01:00:56.580 --> 01:00:58.620
but the logical properties are fixed.

01:00:58.620 --> 01:01:01.680
So disjunction is always disjunction,

01:01:01.680 --> 01:01:04.170
identity is always identity,

01:01:04.170 --> 01:01:07.770
but a property like
being a man in one model,

01:01:07.770 --> 01:01:08.603
it's that property,

01:01:08.603 --> 01:01:11.190
another it's the property of being a woman

01:01:11.190 --> 01:01:15.480
or being an odd number or
being an even number and so on.

01:01:15.480 --> 01:01:20.480
And so what is true in
all models is said to be

01:01:21.120 --> 01:01:24.450
what is logically true,
what couldn't be false,

01:01:24.450 --> 01:01:28.590
what is true due to the
logical structure of sentences

01:01:28.590 --> 01:01:32.460
or logical meaning of sentences,

01:01:32.460 --> 01:01:34.053
which is fixed in all models.

01:01:36.329 --> 01:01:37.162
Now, models are usually a thought to be

01:01:40.860 --> 01:01:44.430
a set theoretical construct.

01:01:44.430 --> 01:01:49.430
And a model consists of a
universe, which is a set

01:01:51.240 --> 01:01:56.240
and then objects and subsets
and relations and so on,

01:01:57.360 --> 01:02:01.950
which represent the properties
that we are talking about

01:02:01.950 --> 01:02:04.377
in a particular sentence.

01:02:04.377 --> 01:02:09.377
And the laws that govern models
are the laws of set theory

01:02:09.960 --> 01:02:12.780
and how many models there are,

01:02:12.780 --> 01:02:17.040
how many possibilities
are represented by models

01:02:17.040 --> 01:02:21.600
is the number of possibilities
that can be represented

01:02:21.600 --> 01:02:23.073
by sets in set theory.

01:02:24.150 --> 01:02:28.560
Now, one distinctive
aspect of set theory is

01:02:28.560 --> 01:02:33.150
that set theory can represent sets

01:02:33.150 --> 01:02:36.543
but it cannot represent
the totality of all sets,

01:02:37.770 --> 01:02:41.370
and one might say that what is really real

01:02:41.370 --> 01:02:44.130
is the totality of all sets.

01:02:44.130 --> 01:02:47.730
And that set theory cannot not represent.

01:02:47.730 --> 01:02:51.480
And so models, set theoretical models

01:02:51.480 --> 01:02:54.270
do not represent this either.

01:02:54.270 --> 01:02:57.510
So truth in all models is in some way

01:02:57.510 --> 01:03:00.480
not truth in all possibilities,

01:03:00.480 --> 01:03:03.123
maybe even not in the real reality.

01:03:04.680 --> 01:03:09.680
Now, in my view this is
not a conclusive argument.

01:03:10.140 --> 01:03:13.680
And the reason is that
we need to distinguish

01:03:13.680 --> 01:03:17.760
between the general idea
and its implementation

01:03:17.760 --> 01:03:22.760
of processification, there is
nothing about the general idea

01:03:23.220 --> 01:03:26.430
of logic that to be true is to be true

01:03:26.430 --> 01:03:29.763
in all formally possible situations.

01:03:30.750 --> 01:03:35.750
And set field, in principle
we could have implemented

01:03:36.690 --> 01:03:38.340
a different field.

01:03:38.340 --> 01:03:41.190
Russell, for example,
in his theory of types,

01:03:41.190 --> 01:03:44.160
didn't use axiomatic set theory

01:03:44.160 --> 01:03:46.830
and didn't have this problem.

01:03:46.830 --> 01:03:51.830
And so I see this just as
a matter of implementation.

01:03:52.260 --> 01:03:57.260
I think the idea is right,
but we still need to find

01:03:58.290 --> 01:04:02.790
an adequate mathematical
theory that will enable us

01:04:02.790 --> 01:04:05.013
to implement it precisely.

01:04:05.850 --> 01:04:08.820
Another point which is relevant is that

01:04:08.820 --> 01:04:12.923
even if we use set
theory, there is a point

01:04:12.923 --> 01:04:15.340
that was emphasized by Mcgee and others

01:04:16.200 --> 01:04:21.000
that in set theory itself there
are reflection principles.

01:04:21.000 --> 01:04:26.000
And these reflection
principles say that even though

01:04:26.340 --> 01:04:30.450
the totality of sets is
not represented by a set,

01:04:30.450 --> 01:04:35.310
there are so many sets and so
many variations between sets

01:04:35.310 --> 01:04:40.050
that it is sufficient to
present any type of structure,

01:04:40.050 --> 01:04:42.720
including the class structure.

01:04:42.720 --> 01:04:46.170
So once again, there are
questions about this point,

01:04:46.170 --> 01:04:51.170
but the first point seems
to me more important that

01:04:51.360 --> 01:04:56.360
the problem is in implementation
and not in principle.

01:04:56.970 --> 01:05:00.243
I wonder what you think about this, Alex?

01:05:01.740 --> 01:05:04.140
- I think you could be right about that,

01:05:04.140 --> 01:05:05.943
I don't disagree with that.

01:05:07.890 --> 01:05:09.840
As I say, it's not
disagreement, it's hesitation

01:05:09.840 --> 01:05:11.260
because I haven't seen

01:05:12.990 --> 01:05:15.450
a convincing implementation of that yet.

01:05:15.450 --> 01:05:19.170
That could be my ignorance,
which is why I hesitate

01:05:19.170 --> 01:05:21.510
to take that step.

01:05:21.510 --> 01:05:26.510
If there were a precise
cogent in implementation

01:05:27.720 --> 01:05:30.510
that undercut all those doubts,

01:05:30.510 --> 01:05:34.053
then I'd join you immediately.

01:05:34.890 --> 01:05:38.820
And there may be one and
maybe you can imagine one,

01:05:38.820 --> 01:05:40.920
but I haven't quite seen one yet,

01:05:40.920 --> 01:05:43.800
so that's why I'm a little hesitant.

01:05:43.800 --> 01:05:46.260
- Yeah, and I think it's partly a matter

01:05:46.260 --> 01:05:50.433
of how you treat philosophical results.

01:05:51.420 --> 01:05:55.230
For me, you can have solid
philosophical results

01:05:55.230 --> 01:05:57.250
even without having yet

01:05:58.267 --> 01:06:00.273
a mathematical implementation for it.

01:06:01.230 --> 01:06:05.577
And the philosophical
result stands at its own,

01:06:05.577 --> 01:06:09.480
but there are many people who
would not agree and would say,

01:06:09.480 --> 01:06:11.850
we actually need an implementation

01:06:11.850 --> 01:06:16.653
in order to substantiate
the philosophical result.

01:06:17.580 --> 01:06:22.208
- So in that way it might be a
matter of temperament almost.

01:06:22.208 --> 01:06:24.570
On the second point about reflection,

01:06:24.570 --> 01:06:25.440
again, I think you're right,

01:06:25.440 --> 01:06:28.290
but again, I mean reflection is sensitive

01:06:28.290 --> 01:06:30.993
to the language of the
set theory and so on.

01:06:32.700 --> 01:06:37.097
- Yeah, it's an open question,
the issue of reflection,

01:06:38.058 --> 01:06:38.891
and interesting.

01:06:38.891 --> 01:06:40.950
- Yes, but thank you for that response

01:06:40.950 --> 01:06:42.821
to my second hesitation.

01:06:42.821 --> 01:06:45.545
- And what was your last point, I forgot?

01:06:45.545 --> 01:06:47.420
- My last point was that
the talk of formal laws

01:06:52.560 --> 01:06:55.173
and formal structure was in a way,

01:06:56.010 --> 01:06:58.800
wasn't sure how much
explanation it was giving you

01:06:58.800 --> 01:07:02.400
because it seemed to be
restating some of the points

01:07:02.400 --> 01:07:04.650
of the infinity of logic,

01:07:04.650 --> 01:07:08.730
but just putting it in
more metaphysical language.

01:07:08.730 --> 01:07:13.730
And so as I see it, it wasn't
giving you very much else

01:07:14.910 --> 01:07:18.000
an explanation, if B
is an explanation of A,

01:07:18.000 --> 01:07:19.920
it's gotta go quite a bit beyond A,

01:07:19.920 --> 01:07:23.970
and it seemed like saying
A the same thing as A,

01:07:23.970 --> 01:07:27.540
but in a much more physical language,

01:07:27.540 --> 01:07:29.253
excuse me, metaphysical language.

01:07:30.570 --> 01:07:34.710
- Yeah, and I think that
this is partly because

01:07:34.710 --> 01:07:36.840
my perspective is a little bit more

01:07:36.840 --> 01:07:40.950
general systemic perspective,
knowledge in general.

01:07:40.950 --> 01:07:43.020
We want to know how the world is.

01:07:43.020 --> 01:07:48.020
So we want to know whether
there are formal properties,

01:07:48.493 --> 01:07:50.803
things in the world
have formal properties,

01:07:50.803 --> 01:07:53.580
and we want to know whether
there are formal laws

01:07:53.580 --> 01:07:55.250
that govern the world.

01:07:55.250 --> 01:07:58.470
This is a question which
is interesting in itself,

01:07:58.470 --> 01:08:01.980
but it's also connection
to the question of truth.

01:08:01.980 --> 01:08:04.440
I mean, we were talking about consequence,

01:08:04.440 --> 01:08:08.790
about implication, but
in infinitistic logic

01:08:08.790 --> 01:08:12.690
of the kind you talk about,
there are also logical truth.

01:08:12.690 --> 01:08:17.690
And the question is whether
logical truth are really true.

01:08:18.150 --> 01:08:22.830
And if they're true in
virtue of what they are true,

01:08:22.830 --> 01:08:27.060
I think that the advantage
of going to the world

01:08:27.060 --> 01:08:30.960
and say that they are
true due to formal laws

01:08:30.960 --> 01:08:32.223
that govern the world,

01:08:32.223 --> 01:08:37.223
give you a straightforward
explanation of why they are true.

01:08:37.620 --> 01:08:41.460
And if you give this
up, it's not clear to me

01:08:41.460 --> 01:08:44.190
how you can establish truth.

01:08:44.190 --> 01:08:47.703
This is the main issue that bothers me.

01:08:48.900 --> 01:08:50.283
- I see that, thank you.

01:08:52.110 --> 01:08:56.550
Yeah, I think I have so
far declined to answer

01:08:56.550 --> 01:08:59.493
that question, and it is a
question that needs an answer.

01:09:00.916 --> 01:09:04.760
I certainly agree with that, yeah.

01:09:04.760 --> 01:09:07.650
- Okay, so I think one
more question has showed up

01:09:07.650 --> 01:09:09.333
in the chat from Ed Brandon.

01:09:10.872 --> 01:09:13.507
And so he asked, I don't think
it's an ignorant question,

01:09:13.507 --> 01:09:16.800
"What is the relation between
valid infinitary arguments,

01:09:16.800 --> 01:09:18.330
Professor Bizo gave,

01:09:18.330 --> 01:09:20.637
and what is called
mathematical induction?"

01:09:22.020 --> 01:09:24.360
- Okay, yeah, so mathematical induction,

01:09:24.360 --> 01:09:26.760
the principle of mathematical
induction is a principle about

01:09:26.760 --> 01:09:30.210
the natural numbers, zero,
one, two, three and so on,

01:09:30.210 --> 01:09:32.070
or some people just start at one.

01:09:32.070 --> 01:09:34.920
And it says that if zero has the property,

01:09:34.920 --> 01:09:38.370
and secondly, if a
number has the property,

01:09:38.370 --> 01:09:40.170
then it's successor has the property,

01:09:40.170 --> 01:09:43.140
from those two facts, you
can infer that every number

01:09:43.140 --> 01:09:44.163
has the property.

01:09:45.090 --> 01:09:49.113
And so that's specific to the
natural numbers, of course,

01:09:50.792 --> 01:09:55.383
it doesn't hold in lots of other domains.

01:09:56.580 --> 01:10:00.810
So for example, if you throw
in the negative integers,

01:10:00.810 --> 01:10:03.960
the principle of mathematical
induction isn't gonna hold,

01:10:03.960 --> 01:10:07.290
because you could have starting
at zero in transmitting,

01:10:07.290 --> 01:10:11.190
but never getting to
the negative integers.

01:10:11.190 --> 01:10:14.640
Valid infinitary arguments
however are completely general

01:10:14.640 --> 01:10:18.093
and apply to any subject
matter whatsoever.

01:10:20.400 --> 01:10:25.400
So it can be about numbers,
natural numbers, real numbers,

01:10:25.440 --> 01:10:30.183
but also about physical
situations, whatever you like.

01:10:33.452 --> 01:10:35.850
Ed, if that does not answer your question,

01:10:35.850 --> 01:10:38.013
then feel free to ask a follow-up.

01:10:41.880 --> 01:10:43.370
- Let me see if there is...

01:10:46.890 --> 01:10:50.733
Yeah, I don't see a follow-up from Ed.

01:10:54.450 --> 01:10:56.283
- I mean, to put it very broadly,

01:10:57.600 --> 01:10:59.550
the principle of mathematical
induction specific

01:10:59.550 --> 01:11:02.520
to arithmetic, to the natural numbers,

01:11:02.520 --> 01:11:05.010
logic, infinitary of finite.

01:11:05.010 --> 01:11:06.810
Gila and I both think it's infinitary,

01:11:06.810 --> 01:11:10.383
applies to any kind of domain.

01:11:11.520 --> 01:11:13.170
- Much broader, right?

01:11:13.170 --> 01:11:14.003
- Yes.

01:11:17.850 --> 01:11:21.250
There is a potential confusion
with the word induction here

01:11:23.070 --> 01:11:25.750
because induction is
used in all sorts of ways

01:11:27.060 --> 01:11:29.520
outside academic uses, you can be inducted

01:11:29.520 --> 01:11:31.863
into the Rock and Roll Hall of Fame.

01:11:33.390 --> 01:11:35.850
But even within philosophy
and mathematics,

01:11:35.850 --> 01:11:37.710
it's used in different ways.

01:11:37.710 --> 01:11:40.920
And so when I was talking
about non-deductive knowledge,

01:11:40.920 --> 01:11:42.750
that's sometimes called
inductive knowledge,

01:11:42.750 --> 01:11:44.250
but that can be a bit confusing.

01:11:44.250 --> 01:11:46.530
So I was keen to just say non-deductive

01:11:46.530 --> 01:11:48.243
to not call it inductive.

01:11:50.820 --> 01:11:55.353
- Great, if there are
no further questions,

01:11:56.400 --> 01:12:00.810
I think we can draw
our session to a close.

01:12:00.810 --> 01:12:05.010
And so let me thank both Gila and Alex

01:12:05.010 --> 01:12:09.360
for a wonderful discussion,
a great lecture yesterday

01:12:09.360 --> 01:12:13.980
and for a really nice discussion today.

01:12:13.980 --> 01:12:17.680
Clarifying points of view,
points of convergence

01:12:18.861 --> 01:12:22.740
and the very few points of
divergence in your views.

01:12:22.740 --> 01:12:24.720
But thank you so much for enriching

01:12:24.720 --> 01:12:26.520
and teaching us all so much.

01:12:26.520 --> 01:12:30.900
Yeah, so let's give a round of applause

01:12:30.900 --> 01:12:33.060
to our speakers today.

01:12:33.060 --> 01:12:35.760
And yeah, we're really deeply grateful

01:12:35.760 --> 01:12:37.500
to both of you for coming.

01:12:37.500 --> 01:12:39.060
Thanks, bye.

01:12:39.060 --> 01:12:40.507
- Thanks, bye.
- Bye-bye.

